"""Authored depth packs for digital logic and computer architecture."""
from . import P, E

P('1-boolean-algebra','Boolean algebra',r'''
Think of a Boolean expression as a set of input assignments on which the output is true. OR forms a union, AND an intersection, and complement exchanges true and false assignments within the stated input space. This explains absorption: intersecting A with another condition cannot add an assignment outside A. When simplifying, replace an entire subexpression using an identity rather than treating its symbols like ordinary numbers. Complementation reverses every AND/OR level, so parentheses are part of the reasoning. A useful independent check is a truth table or a counterexample assignment; matching a few convenient rows does not establish an identity for all inputs.
''',
E('fundamental','Complement F=(A+B)C using Boolean laws.',r'Complement the outer product::\bar F=\overline{A+B}+\bar C::De Morgan turns the AND of two factors into an OR of their complements.~Complement the inner sum::\overline{A+B}=\bar A\bar B::Both literals are complemented and OR becomes AND.~Substitute::\bar F=\bar A\bar B+\bar C::The grouping identifies which operations have been reversed.', 'The complement is ¬A¬B+¬C.','For C=0 the complement is one; for C=1 it is one exactly when A=B=0, precisely the cases where F is zero.'),
E('application','Show that the consensus term BC is redundant in F=AB+¬AC+BC.',r'Split by A::BC=ABC+\bar ABC::Multiplying by A+¬A does not change BC.~Absorb split pieces::AB+ABC=AB,\quad\bar AC+\bar ABC=\bar AC::Each piece is contained in one existing term.~Keep necessary terms::F=AB+\bar AC::The removed bridge term adds no true assignment.', 'F=AB+¬AC.','If BC=1, A is either one and AB=1, or zero and ¬AC=1; thus BC never uniquely makes the output true.'),
E('trap','Does A+B=A+C allow Boolean cancellation of A to conclude B=C?',r'Test a dominating input::A=1::OR with one forces both sides to one independently of the other operand.~Choose unequal operands::B=0,\quad C=1::These satisfy the original equality while differing.~Evaluate both claims::1+0=1+1=1,\quad0\ne1::Equality of the compound expressions does not imply equality of their operands.', 'No; Boolean OR has no general cancellation law.','The single assignment A=1,B=0,C=1 disproves the proposed implication without disputing ordinary arithmetic cancellation.'))

P('1-boolean-algebra','Algebraic technique',r'''
Algebraic minimization becomes easier when a target identity determines the next transformation. Search for common factors, complementary cases, or a term already covered by another term. Factoring exposes structure, while expansion is useful when it creates an absorption or consensus pattern; expansion is not automatically a step backward. Record the reason for each equality and retain the original variable conventions. A simplified expression should preserve every specified truth-table row. After correctness, compare the cost the question actually asks for: two product terms, three literals, and two physical gates are different measurements. Algebra can prove equivalence without proving a unique minimum implementation.
''',
E('fundamental','Simplify (A+B)(A+¬B) by an algebraic identity.',r'Use repeated-factor identity::(X+Y)(X+Z)=X+YZ::This follows by distribution and absorption.~Substitute factors::(A+B)(A+\bar B)=A+B\bar B::The repeated literal A is retained once.~Use complement::A+B\bar B=A+0=A::A variable AND its complement is always zero.', 'The result is A.','For A=1 both factors are one; for A=0 their product is B¬B=0, so the result equals A on all four rows.'),
E('application','Minimize F=AB+A¬B+¬AC and compare literal counts of the written SOPs.',r'Combine complementary branches::AB+A\bar B=A(B+\bar B)=A::The first pair covers both values of B when A is true.~Apply absorption with complement::A+\bar AC=A+C::When A is zero the expression reduces to C; when A is one both sides are one.~Count the written literals::L_{before}=6,\quad L_{after}=2::The minimized SOP contains the two one-literal terms A and C.', 'F=A+C; the written literal count falls from six to two.','Case-splitting on A verifies the identity. The count is of literal occurrences, without assuming a particular physical gate library.'),
E('trap','A proposed simplification replaces AB+AC by A(B+C) and then by A. Is the last equality valid?',r'Accept justified factoring::AB+AC=A(B+C)::Distribution proves the first replacement.~Find a restrictive case::A=1,\quad B=C=0::The parenthesized condition is not always one.~Compare outputs::A(B+C)=0,\quad A=1::Dropping the factor adds an unintended true row.', 'Factoring is valid; deleting B+C is invalid.','The assignment 100 is false in the original expression and true in the proposed final expression.'))

P('1-boolean-sop-rows','SOP',r'''
Canonical SOP records a function by enumerating true rows; a minimized SOP records larger regions of true rows. These representations describe the same function at different levels of detail. Translating a row into a minterm requires all variables, whereas a simplified product can omit a varying variable. Once a correct SOP is available, a two-level NAND implementation follows by complementing each product and then NANDing those complemented results. This works because the outer De Morgan transformation restores the OR of products. State whether input complements already exist before counting gates, and do not confuse a compact product with a canonical minterm of an incompletely specified variable set.
''',
E('fundamental','For variable order A,B, form the canonical SOP of a function true only at rows 01 and 10.',r'Translate first row::01\to\bar AB::The zero bit complements A and the one bit retains B.~Translate second row::10\to A\bar B::Every product must contain both variables.~OR true-row products::F=\bar AB+A\bar B::The result is a canonical sum of the two minterms.', 'F=¬AB+A¬B, or Σm(1,2).','The products are mutually exclusive and true exactly when the two inputs differ, giving the XOR truth table.'),
E('application','Realize the SOP F=AB+¬AC using only two-input NAND gates; complementary inputs are not supplied.',r'Generate complement::n_A=NAND(A,A)=\bar A::One gate supplies the missing literal.~Complement products::u=NAND(A,B),\quad v=NAND(n_A,C)::These outputs are ¬AB and ¬(¬AC).~Restore SOP::F=NAND(u,v)=AB+\bar AC::An outer NAND applies De Morgan, producing the desired OR.', 'Four two-input NAND gates are sufficient.','For A=1 the circuit gives B, and for A=0 it gives C, matching the two products independently.'),
E('trap','Is F=A already a canonical SOP when the declared variables are A and B?',r'Identify missing variable::::The product A is an SOP term, but a canonical minterm must contain both A and B.~Expand the missing cases::A=A(B+\bar B)::Insert a factor equal to one without changing the function.~Distribute::F=AB+A\bar B=\Sigma m(2,3)::The two true rows are explicitly represented.', 'A is a simplified SOP, not a canonical SOP in variables A,B; the canonical form is A¬B+AB.','Both expanded products require A=1 and cover the two possible values of B, while neither covers an A=0 row.'))

P('1-boolean-forms','POS',r'''
A POS expression excludes false assignments by ANDing sum factors. Each canonical maxterm becomes zero at exactly one row, so any listed false row forces the entire product to zero. This is why the literal convention reverses relative to minterms: a zero row bit uses an ordinary literal inside the OR, and a one row bit uses a complement. Simplified POS factors can exclude several false rows at once. NOR gates naturally implement POS by first complementing each sum and then NORing those complements. Always state the variable order and whether the requested indices belong to the function or its complement; confusing true and false index sets changes the function.
''',
E('fundamental','For A,B, construct canonical POS when F is zero at 00 and 11 only.',r'Write zero-row factors::M_0=A+B,\quad M_3=\bar A+\bar B::Each sum is zero on its corresponding row.~Multiply factors::F=(A+B)(\bar A+\bar B)::A zero in either factor makes the function zero.~Check the other rows::::At 01 and 10 each factor has a true literal, so their product is one.', 'F=(A+B)(¬A+¬B)=ΠM(0,3).','The outputs in row order 00,01,10,11 are 0,1,1,0, matching XOR.'),
E('application','Implement F=(A+B)(C+D) using two-input NOR gates only.',r'Complement each sum::u=NOR(A,B)=\overline{A+B},\quad v=NOR(C,D)=\overline{C+D}::The first level produces complemented POS factors.~Combine by NOR::F=NOR(u,v)=\overline{u+v}::The second level complements their OR.~Apply De Morgan::F=(A+B)(C+D)::The result is exactly the required product of sums.', 'Three two-input NOR gates implement the function.','The final output is one only when u=v=0, which means each original sum has at least one true input.'),
E('trap','For order A,B,C, a student writes maxterm M₅ as A¬BC. Find the correct maxterm and explain the error.',r'Decode the index::5=101_2::The maxterm must vanish at A=1,B=0,C=1.~Choose zero-valued literals::M_5=\bar A+B+\bar C::Every OR input evaluates to zero on row 101.~Test the proposed product::A\bar BC=1\text{ at }101::That expression is a minterm selecting the row, rather than a maxterm excluding it.', 'M₅=¬A+B+¬C; the student used SOP minterm rules.','At any different row at least one maxterm literal becomes one, whereas the proposed minterm is zero on every different row.'))

P('1-kmap-tabular-maps','Karnaugh map',r'''
The geometry of a Karnaugh map encodes a binary adjacency relation rather than ordinary numeric order. Gray-code neighbors differ in one bit, including the wrap across opposite edges. A legal rectangle has a power-of-two number of cells and changes every eliminated variable through all its combinations. The retained literals are exactly the variables constant throughout that rectangle. For SOP group ones; for POS group zeros and choose a sum factor that vanishes on the group. Large-looking diagonal patterns may not be rectangles at all. Check the binary assignments represented by a proposed group and verify both required coverage and exclusion of forbidden rows before claiming a simplification.
''',
E('fundamental','In a three-variable map, group the ones at minterms 0 and 4; all other rows are zero.',r'Write binary rows::0=000,\quad4=100::Only A changes between the two true assignments.~Use edge adjacency::::In the Gray-code map these cells are adjacent across the appropriate boundary, so a two-cell group is legal.~Keep fixed literals::F=\bar B\bar C::Eliminate A while retaining the zero values of B and C.', 'F=¬B¬C.','The resulting product covers exactly 000 and 100; it includes no required-zero row.'),
E('application','Use zero grouping to minimize a four-variable function whose only zeros are 0,2,8,10; variable order is A,B,C,D.',r'Inspect zero rows::0000,0010,1000,1010::A and C vary through all combinations while B=D=0.~Form a wrapped four-cell group::::The corner/edge arrangement is one legal rectangle in Gray-code order, eliminating A and C.~Write a POS factor::F=B+D::This sum is zero exactly when B and D are both zero.', 'The minimum POS is the single factor B+D.','There are four assignments with B=D=0; these are precisely the specified zero indices, and every other assignment makes the sum one.'),
E('trap','Can two one-cells at 00 and 11 in a two-variable map be grouped into a term with no literals?',r'Check bit distance::00\to11\text{ changes two bits}::These cells are diagonal rather than adjacent.~Check what a zero-literal term means::F=1::Eliminating both variables would cover all four rows.~Test an omitted zero row::A=0,\quad B=1::The original function is zero there, but the proposed constant is one.', 'No; the two diagonal cells remain separate minterms, yielding ¬A¬B+AB.','The correct expression has outputs 1,0,0,1; grouping the diagonal would incorrectly set the middle two rows to one.'))

P('1-kmap-tabular-tabular','Tabular method',r'''
The tabular method represents groups as binary patterns with dashes. A dash means that both values of that variable are included, so each extra dash doubles the represented rows. Combining two patterns is legal only if their dash positions match and exactly one remaining bit differs. Keep a record of the minterms covered by every pattern, because duplicate combinations can arise through different routes. Mark combined terms and retain uncombined ones as prime candidates. Don’t-cares may enlarge combinations but must not become mandatory chart columns. The method separates generating prime implicants from selecting a cover; stopping after a first combination can leave a correct but unnecessarily expensive expression.
''',
E('fundamental','Run tabular combination for F(A,B,C)=Σm(4,5), with all other rows zero.',r'Group binary terms::4=100,\quad5=101::The terms lie in adjacent one-count groups.~Combine one-bit difference::100,101\to10-::The last position varies while A=1 and B=0 remain fixed.~Stop and interpret::F=A\bar B::No other true row permits enlarging this pattern.', 'The prime pattern is 10−, corresponding to A¬B.','Expanding its dash gives only rows 100 and 101, so the expression covers precisely the two required minterms.'),
E('application','For F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,1,2,3,6,7), find the final tabular cover.',r'Generate first-round pairs::::Rows 0,1,2,3 form 00−,01−,0−0 and 0−1; rows 2,3,6,7 form −10,−11,01− and 11−.~Combine matching dash patterns::00-,01-\to0--,\quad-10,-11\to-1-::The two four-row primes are ¬A and B.~Cover the true set::F=\bar A+B::The union of their coverage is {0,1,2,3,6,7}.', 'A minimum cover is ¬A+B.','At rows 4 and 5, A=1 and B=0, making the expression zero; every other row satisfies at least one term.'),
E('trap','May patterns 0−1 and −01 be combined into −−1?',r'Check dash alignment::::The first pattern varies B; the second varies A. Their dash positions do not match.~Enumerate actual coverage::0-1\to\{1,3\},\quad-01\to\{1,5\}::Their union has three rows, not a four-row binary cube.~Expand the proposed pattern::--1\to\{1,3,5,7\}::It adds minterm 7, which could be a required zero.', 'No; the proposed combination is invalid unless further terms independently justify the missing row.','A function true only at 1,3,5 is a counterexample: the combined pattern wrongly turns row 7 on.'))

P('1-kmap-tabular-prime-chart','Prime implicants',r'''
Prime implicants are maximal safe groups, while essential primes are unavoidable members of a cover. These are different claims: a prime may be omitted when other primes cover all its required rows. In a chart, unique coverage proves essentiality and a lower bound on the number of selected terms. When no column is unique, choose a cover by an explicit criterion rather than assuming the absence of essentials means the function is empty. A prime chart can contain ties and multiple optimal covers. Don’t-care rows help determine which groups are maximal but are excluded from mandatory coverage. Verify minimality using forced columns or a coverage lower bound, not merely the appearance of a concise expression.
''',
E('fundamental','For F(A,B)=Σm(0,1), determine its prime implicants and essential primes.',r'Combine true rows::00,01\to0-::Both true assignments have A=0, so the largest safe group is ¬A.~Rule out enlargement::::Removing A would include required-zero rows 10 and 11, so ¬A is prime.~Inspect chart uniqueness::::Both required columns are covered only by ¬A, making that prime essential.', 'The only prime implicant is ¬A, and it is essential.','The candidate covers both true rows and no zeros; every valid cover must cover minterm 0, which has no alternative prime.'),
E('application','For F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,1,2,5,6,7), the prime chart contains pairs {0,1},{0,2},{1,5},{2,6},{5,7},{6,7}. Identify essential primes and a minimum SOP.',r'Inspect column counts::::Each required minterm belongs to exactly two listed pairs, so no prime is essential.~Choose a complete cover::\{0,1\}\cup\{2,6\}\cup\{5,7\}=\{0,1,2,5,6,7\}::The corresponding terms are ¬A¬B, B¬C and AC.~Prove the lower bound::::Every prime covers two required minterms, so at least three primes are needed to cover six distinct columns.', 'There are no essential primes; F=¬A¬B+B¬C+AC is a minimum three-term SOP.','The three selected pairs are disjoint and cover all six ones. Each listed pair is a maximal legal binary group because any four-cell enlargement would include zero row 3 or 4.'),
E('trap','For F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,1,2,5), is prime ¬A¬B necessarily required because it covers two minterms?',r'List its coverage::\bar A\bar B\to\{0,1\}::Being a two-cell prime does not establish unique coverage.~Find alternatives::\bar A\bar C\to\{0,2\},\quad\bar BC\to\{1,5\}::Each row of the first prime is also covered elsewhere.~Form a cover without it::F=\bar A\bar C+\bar BC::The alternatives cover every required minterm.', 'No; ¬A¬B is prime but not essential and may be omitted.','The two alternative sets unite to {0,1,2,5}; minterms 2 and 5 force those alternatives, so adding ¬A¬B would be redundant.'))

P('1-kmap-tabular-maps','Dont cares',r'''
A don’t-care is freedom in a specification, not a third physical logic value. The implemented circuit still returns zero or one at that input, but either output is accepted because the system promises that the input is irrelevant or unreachable. Use optional rows only when they enlarge a useful group or reduce cost; covering all of them is unnecessary. The promise matters: if an invalid code can occur through a fault or transient, the simplified circuit may behave differently from another legal realization. List required ones, required zeros and optional rows separately. Check every required row after minimization, and explain any operating assumption that makes the optional rows genuinely unconstrained.
''',
E('fundamental','F(A,B)=1 at minterm 1, has don’t-care 3, and is zero at 0 and 2. Choose a simple realization.',r'Identify available group::1=01,\quad3=11::The optional row can join the required one.~Eliminate varying bit::A\text{ varies},\quad B=1::The two-row group retains only B.~Check required outputs::F=B::Rows 0 and 2 have B=0, while required row 1 has B=1.', 'F=B is a valid realization.','At optional row 3 the output is one, which is allowed; the specified rows remain correct.'),
E('application','A BCD input A,B,C,D must output one for odd digits 1,3,5,7,9; digits 10–15 are don’t-cares. Minimize the odd-digit detector.',r'Interpret valid digits::::For every valid BCD digit, oddness is exactly the least significant bit D.~Extend into optional region::F=D::This also marks 11,13,15 as one, but those inputs are unconstrained.~Check all valid codes::::Even digits 0,2,4,6,8 have D=0 and odd digits have D=1, so one wire implements the valid specification.', 'The detector is F=D, assuming inputs are valid BCD or invalid-code outputs are unconstrained.','Enumerating digits 0 through 9 confirms parity; no assertion is made about a required safe output for codes 10 through 15.'),
E('trap','A student declares a required-zero row a don’t-care to create a larger K-map group. For F=Σm(1), required zero 3, is F=B valid in variables A,B?',r'Inspect the specification::::Only 01 is true, while 11 is explicitly false and cannot be reclassified.~Evaluate the shortcut::F(1,1)=B=1::The proposed wire turns the required-zero row on.~Keep the needed literal::F=\bar AB::A complement separates row 01 from forbidden row 11.', 'No; F=¬AB is required when row 3 is specified zero.','The simplified wire is legal only for a different specification where row 3 is optional; the original truth table forbids it.'))

P('1-combinational-adders','Combinational circuits',r'''
A combinational design is a function of its present inputs, so start with a precise input/output contract before choosing gates. Translate verbal conditions into a truth table or equations, simplify, then verify the implementation on boundary cases. No clock edge or remembered state is available unless storage is explicitly added. Gate propagation delay means output changes are not instantaneous, but delay by itself does not make a circuit sequential. Multiple paths can briefly disagree and produce hazards even when their final truth table is correct. Distinguish functional correctness from timing correctness, and state active levels or validity assumptions whenever signal names such as enable, alarm or ready could otherwise be ambiguous.
''',
E('fundamental','An active-high alarm is required when enable E=1 and at least one of sensors A,B=1. Derive its Boolean function.',r'Translate sensor condition::S=A+B::Either active sensor is enough to request an alarm.~Apply enable::Y=E(A+B)::The enable condition must hold simultaneously with the sensor condition.~Check disabled behavior::E=0\Rightarrow Y=0::All sensor combinations are suppressed when disabled.', 'Y=E(A+B).','For E=1 the four A,B rows yield 0,1,1,1; for E=0 all four outputs are zero, giving the required eight-row truth table.'),
E('application','Design a combinational three-input majority voter that outputs one when at least two of A,B,C are one.',r'List qualifying pairs::::Any two true inputs contain pair AB, AC or BC; a three-one input satisfies all pairs.~Write the SOP::Y=AB+AC+BC::OR the conditions for each possible true pair.~Verify low-count cases::::With zero or one input true, every two-input product is zero, so the output remains zero.', 'Y=AB+AC+BC, implementable using three AND gates feeding an OR gate.','For input weights 0,1,2,3, the output is respectively 0,0,1,1; this verifies every truth-table row by symmetry.'),
E('trap','In a two-level implementation F=AB+¬AC, let B=C=1 and A switch 0→1. Can a momentary zero occur even though the function remains one?',r'Check steady values::F=A+\bar A=1::Both stable input states require output one.~Consider unequal delays::::If the ¬AC path falls before AB rises, both OR inputs can temporarily be zero.~Add a consensus bridge::F=AB+\bar AC+BC::The BC term stays one during this input change and can remove the static-one hazard under the two-level delay model.', 'Yes; a functional identity does not guarantee hazard-free timing. Adding BC provides a stable bridge.','BC is algebraically redundant at settled inputs, but with B=C=1 it maintains a high OR input while the two A-dependent paths switch.'))

P('1-combinational-adders','Adders',r'''
Binary addition can be understood one column at a time: count the true operand and carry-in bits, write the parity as the sum bit and propagate a carry when at least two are true. A full adder therefore combines a three-input parity function with a majority carry function. Chaining full adders creates word addition, where carry propagation determines timing. Unsigned carry and signed overflow answer different questions about the same bit result. To analyze signed overflow, inspect operand signs and result sign, or compare carries entering and leaving the sign bit. State width, extension rules and carry-in before calculating; extra result bits should be interpreted deliberately rather than silently discarded.
''',
E('fundamental','A full adder receives A=1, B=1 and carry-in 1. Find sum and carry-out.',r'Count the input total::1+1+1=3::A one-bit column can produce a low result bit and a carry of weight two.~Compute parity::S=A\oplus B\oplus C_{in}=1::Three true inputs have odd parity.~Compute majority carry::C_{out}=AB+AC_{in}+BC_{in}=1::At least two inputs are one.', 'Sum=1 and carry-out=1, representing binary 11.','The weighted outputs satisfy S+2Cout=1+2=3, equal to the input total.'),
E('application','A four-bit carry-lookahead adder has generate gᵢ=aᵢbᵢ and propagate pᵢ=aᵢ XOR bᵢ. Derive carry c₂ from c₀, g₀,g₁,p₀,p₁.',r'Write first carry::c_1=g_0+p_0c_0::Bit zero generates a carry itself or passes the incoming carry.~Write second recurrence::c_2=g_1+p_1c_1::Bit one uses the same generate/propagate rule.~Expand dependence::c_2=g_1+p_1g_0+p_1p_0c_0::Substitution exposes a direct combinational expression from initial inputs.', 'c₂=g₁+p₁g₀+p₁p₀c₀.','If bit one generates, c₂=1; otherwise it must propagate either the carry generated at bit zero or an initial carry propagated through both bits.'),
E('trap','Add 1111 and 0001 in four bits. Does the carry-out imply signed overflow?',r'Add the bit patterns::1111+0001=10000::The retained result is 0000 and carry-out is one.~Interpret signed operands::-1+1=0::The operands have opposite signs and the exact signed answer fits the range.~Interpret unsigned operands::15+1=16::The exact unsigned answer requires a fifth bit.', 'Carry-out=1; unsigned overflow occurs, but signed overflow does not.','Carries into and out of the sign position are both one, so their XOR is zero, agreeing with the signed-sign test.'))

P('1-combinational-selection','Multiplexers',r'''
A multiplexer implements a conditional choice: fixing the select bits leaves one data input connected to the output. To realize a function, choose selects and calculate its residual function separately for every select assignment. This is Shannon decomposition, and the residual can be a constant, a remaining variable, its complement or a smaller circuit. The number of data inputs is a power of two for a complete binary select space. Data width is independent of the number of choices, so a four-way eight-bit selector is eight parallel four-to-one muxes with shared selects. Verify wiring by branch evaluation; confusing select order or treating a data input as a select creates a valid circuit for the wrong function.
''',
E('fundamental','A 4:1 mux has select bits S₁S₀=10 and inputs I₀=0,I₁=1,I₂=1,I₃=0. Find the output.',r'Interpret select order::i=2S_1+S_0=2::S₁ is declared the most significant select.~Choose the input::Y=I_2::Exactly one data branch is selected.~Read its value::I_2=1::Unselected input values cannot affect the settled output.', 'The output is one.','Changing any input other than I₂ leaves the output unchanged while the selects remain 10.'),
E('application','Implement F(A,B,C)=Σm(1,2,6,7) with a 4:1 mux selected by A,B; A is the high select.',r'Evaluate first two branches::AB=00\Rightarrow F=C,\quad AB=01\Rightarrow F=\bar C::Rows 0,1 and 2,3 determine the residuals.~Evaluate remaining branches::AB=10\Rightarrow F=0,\quad AB=11\Rightarrow F=1::Neither row 4,5 is true; both rows 6,7 are true.~Wire data inputs::I_0=C,\ I_1=\bar C,\ I_2=0,\ I_3=1::Each select branch now reproduces its two-row truth table.', 'Data inputs are C, ¬C, 0 and 1 in index order.','The selected branches yield true rows 1,2,6,7 and false rows 0,3,4,5, establishing all eight cases.'),
E('trap','Can a 2:1 mux preserve yesterday’s selected data value when its current inputs and select change?',r'Write its current-input equation::Y=\bar SI_0+SI_1::The formula contains no stored state.~Choose a new selected value::::If S becomes one and I₁ is zero, the settled output is zero regardless of its previous value.~Identify required storage::::A register or latch must be added if the design must retain earlier data.', 'No; an ordinary mux is combinational and does not remember earlier selected values.','Two histories ending with the same present S,I₀,I₁ must produce the same settled Y; a memory element can distinguish those histories.'))

P('1-combinational-decoder-minterms','Decoders',r'''
A decoder separates input codes into distinct output lines. Its active-level convention determines whether the selected line becomes one or zero, and an enable determines what happens when decoding is disabled. This makes polarity part of the logic, not a cosmetic wiring label. Hierarchical decoding can expand input width by using high bits to enable one lower-bit decoder bank. In minterm realization, combine exactly the selected lines corresponding to required true codes, then account for any output inversion. Address-decoding applications also require that inactive banks release a shared bus; the decoder selects a device but does not itself guarantee correct data-bus electrical behavior or store an earlier selection.
''',
E('fundamental','An enabled active-high 2-to-4 decoder receives inputs A,B=10, with A the high bit. Find Y₀ through Y₃.',r'Decode the index::i=2A+B=2::The binary input selects line two.~Assert the selected line::Y_2=1::The enabled active-high convention makes that output true.~Deassert the others::Y_0=Y_1=Y_3=0::Distinct minterms cannot simultaneously match one input code.', 'Outputs in index order are 0,0,1,0.','The output vector contains exactly one high bit and its index equals the input code.'),
E('application','Use an active-low 3-to-8 decoder, kept enabled, to implement F=Σm(0,3,5). Let Zᵢ be the active-low output lines.',r'Interpret decoder polarity::Z_i=\bar m_i::The matching code drives its line low, unlike an active-high minterm output.~Choose listed lines::F=\overline{Z_0Z_3Z_5}::A NAND output becomes one when any selected line is low.~Verify unlisted input::::At input 010, Z₀,Z₃,Z₅ are all high, so the NAND output is zero.', 'NAND Z₀,Z₃,Z₅ to obtain F.','For each listed code one NAND input is zero, giving F=1; for every unlisted code all three inputs are one, giving F=0.'),
E('trap','Two active-high 2-to-4 decoders form a 3-to-8 decoder. Can both enables simply be tied high if A is the added high-order bit?',r'Count simultaneous selections::::With both enables high, each lower decoder selects one output for the same B,C code, yielding two high outputs.~Use the high bit for bank selection::E_{low}=\bar A,\quad E_{high}=A::Exactly one decoder bank is enabled.~Assign output numbering::::The low bank represents indices 0–3 and the high bank 4–7, each selected internally by B,C.', 'No; complementary enables derived from A are required for one-hot 3-to-8 behavior.','At input 101 only the high bank is enabled and its local line 1 selects global output 5; the low bank remains inactive.'))

P('1-sequential-state','Sequential circuits',r'''
Stored state compresses the useful part of input history. The same current input can therefore produce different behavior depending on the state reached earlier. Define what each state means before assigning its binary code; otherwise the transition table may have no clear connection to the intended memory. In a synchronous circuit, every register computes its next value from the common pre-edge state and then updates together. Feedback through a register is not the same as instantaneous combinational substitution. Specify reset state, active clock edge and whether outputs are observed before or after that edge. A complete trace lists old state, sampled input, next state and output under the chosen convention.
''',
E('fundamental','A parity register starts Q=0 and updates Q⁺=Q XOR X on each rising edge. Trace sampled inputs 1,0,1.',r'First edge::Q^+=0\oplus1=1::One sampled one makes the accumulated parity odd.~Second edge::Q^+=1\oplus0=1::A zero does not change the count of ones.~Third edge::Q^+=1\oplus1=0::The second sampled one restores even parity.', 'After-edge states are 1,1,0; final parity is even.','The sequence contains two ones, and two toggles return the initial zero state.'),
E('application','A synchronous two-bit shift register has A⁺=X and B⁺=A. It starts AB=00; find states after inputs 1,0,1.',r'Apply first edge::AB:00\to10::A receives one, while B receives the old A=0.~Apply second edge::AB:10\to01::A receives zero and B receives the preceding A=1.~Apply third edge::AB:01\to10::A receives one while B receives the preceding zero.', 'The states are 10,01,10; B delays the sampled input by one additional edge.','After each edge the pair stores the latest two sampled bits in order, with reset supplying the missing initial older bit.'),
E('trap','Why is it wrong to update A first and then use that new A to compute B in the shift register A⁺=X, B⁺=A?',r'Choose a distinguishing edge::A=0,\quad X=1::Old and new values of A differ on this edge.~Compute simultaneous updates::A^+=1,\quad B^+=0::Both D inputs are determined before the common clock edge.~Compare sequential substitution::B^+=A^+=1::Using the new A removes the intended one-edge delay.', 'The new B must be the old A; register updates are simultaneous under this model.','Starting AB=00 and sampling one must produce 10, whereas the incorrect procedure produces 11.'))

P('1-sequential-storage','Flip flops',r'''
A flip-flop’s characteristic equation describes its next stored bit from present state and sampled inputs. Its excitation table answers the reverse question: which input settings cause a required state transition? Keep those two directions distinct when designing a counter or state machine. Clock-to-Q, setup and hold describe different parts of timing: clock-to-Q follows the launching edge, setup precedes the receiving edge, and hold constrains the earliest arriving new data. Maximum path delay sets a minimum clock period, while minimum path delay determines hold safety. These examples assume edge-triggered devices and zero clock skew unless stated; level-sensitive latches require a different timing analysis.
''',
E('fundamental','A JK flip-flop starts Q=0. At successive rising edges apply (J,K)=(1,0),(1,1),(0,0). Trace Q.',r'Set on first edge::(J,K)=(1,0)\Rightarrow Q^+=1::This pair sets the stored bit regardless of its previous value.~Toggle on second edge::(1,1)\Rightarrow Q^+=\bar Q=0::Both asserted inputs toggle the old one.~Hold on third edge::(0,0)\Rightarrow Q^+=Q=0::Neither set nor reset is requested.', 'The states after the three edges are 1,0,0.','The characteristic equation Q⁺=J¬Q+¬KQ gives the same value at each of the three old-state/input combinations.'),
E('application','A register-to-register path has maximum clock-to-Q 2 ns, logic delay 5 ns and receiving setup 1 ns. With zero skew, find the largest safe clock frequency.',r'Form setup constraint::T_{clk}\ge t_{cq,max}+t_{logic,max}+t_{setup}::Data must launch, cross the logic and settle before the next receiving edge.~Add delays::T_{clk}\ge2+5+1=8\ ns::All three maximum-path components consume the available period.~Invert period::f_{max}=1/(8\ ns)=125\ MHz::Frequency is the reciprocal of the minimum period.', 'The setup-limited maximum frequency is 125 MHz.','At an 8 ns period the data arrives at 7 ns and has the required 1 ns setup margin before the next edge; hold must still be checked separately.'),
E('trap','Minimum clock-to-Q is 0.5 ns and minimum logic delay 0.2 ns; the receiver needs hold 1 ns, with zero skew. Will a slower clock fix the hold violation?',r'Compute earliest arrival::t_{early}=0.5+0.2=0.7\ ns::The new data can reach the receiver this soon after the launching edge.~Compare hold requirement::0.7<1::The receiver needs the old data retained for another 0.3 ns.~Locate the relevant edge::::Hold is checked against the same sampling edge, not the next cycle, so increasing the period does not increase this margin.', 'No; at least 0.3 ns of additional minimum delay or another valid hold remedy is needed.','The inequality t_cq,min+t_logic,min≥t_hold remains violated regardless of the interval to the next rising edge.'))

P('1-sequential-modulo-counter','Counters',r'''
Counter design separates the desired state cycle from the storage mechanism that realizes it. Binary, one-hot ring and Johnson counters have different state encodings and different numbers of usable states, even when they use the same number of flip-flops. Derive next-state or excitation equations from the intended sequence rather than assuming every collection of toggling bits counts correctly. For a synchronous binary incrementer, a higher bit toggles when all lower old-state bits are one. Non-power-of-two counters need an explicit wrap condition and possibly recovery rules for unused encodings. Reset is part of the operating contract; without a valid initialization, some feedback counters can remain permanently trapped outside their intended cycle.
''',
E('fundamental','How many flip-flops are needed for a binary modulo-ten counter, and what follows state 1001?',r'Find required width::b=\lceil\log_2 10\rceil=4::Three bits encode only eight distinct states.~Interpret terminal state::1001_2=9::This is the tenth state when counting starts at zero.~Apply modulo wrap::9\to0::The required next code is 0000 rather than ordinary binary 1010.', 'Four flip-flops; 1001 transitions to 0000.','The cycle contains exactly codes 0 through 9 before returning to its start; six other four-bit encodings need separate treatment if recovery is required.'),
E('application','For a synchronous three-bit binary up-counter built from T flip-flops, derive T₀,T₁,T₂ and trace old state Q₂Q₁Q₀=011.',r'Determine toggle conditions::T_0=1,\quad T_1=Q_0,\quad T_2=Q_1Q_0::A bit toggles precisely when every lower old-state bit is one.~Evaluate old state::011\Rightarrow(T_2,T_1,T_0)=(1,1,1)::All three toggle conditions hold before the edge.~Update together::011\oplus111=100::The shared edge produces the next binary integer.', 'T₀=1, T₁=Q₀, T₂=Q₁Q₀; state 011 becomes 100.','The weighted value changes from 3 to 4, and simultaneous XOR updates agree with binary increment rather than ripple intermediates.'),
E('trap','A four-bit ring counter rotates its bits left each edge and is initialized to 0000. Will it generate four one-hot states?',r'Apply rotation to reset::::Rotating 0000 leaves every bit zero.~Repeat the recurrence::::Since no update introduces a one, every later state is also 0000.~Provide a valid seed::::Initializing to 0001 instead yields 0001→0010→0100→1000→0001 under the stated rotation.', 'No; zero initialization traps this ring counter at zero. A one-hot seed is needed.','Rotation preserves the number of one bits, so a zero-one-count state can never enter the intended one-one-count cycle.'))

P('1-sequential-moore-detector','Moore',r'''
A Moore output is attached to a state meaning, not an input-labelled transition. This makes the output a direct function of the stored state code, although physical decoding delay still exists after a state change. When a detector recognizes a complete pattern, create a state whose meaning includes that completion and assign its output there. The outgoing transitions should preserve any useful suffix for subsequent matches. A state table can be checked by applying each input to the meaning of the current state, rather than memorizing arrows. Specify the observation point: outputs just before an edge correspond to old state, while outputs after state settling correspond to next state; comparing unspecified conventions produces misleading latency claims.
''',
E('fundamental','A Moore machine starts in N/output 0. Input 1 toggles between N and Y/output 1; input 0 holds state. Trace inputs 1,1,0, observing outputs after each edge.',r'First toggle::::N becomes Y on the first sampled one, so the output becomes one.~Second toggle::::Y becomes N on the second sampled one, so the output becomes zero.~Final hold::::The sampled zero retains N and therefore retains output zero.', 'After-edge outputs are 1,0,0.','The output is exactly the parity of the number of sampled ones, and the zero changes neither state nor output.'),
E('application','Build a Moore detector for 011 with states N=no prefix, A=0, B=01, M=complete 011. Only M outputs one. Give transitions and trace 011011 after edges.',r'Derive partial-prefix transitions::::N:0→A,1→N; A:0→A,1→B; B:0→A,1→M. Each state retains the longest suffix useful for 011.~Continue after recognition::::From M, 0→A and 1→N; the next input can start a new useful suffix immediately.~Trace sampled sequence::::011011 produces A,B,M,A,B,M, so only positions 3 and 6 have output one.', 'The transition rules above give after-edge outputs 0,0,1,0,0,1.','The two occurrences of 011 end at positions 3 and 6; every output-one state corresponds to a completed occurrence and no others do.'),
E('trap','A synchronous Moore machine remains in state M/output 1 while its external input changes between edges. Must its logical output change immediately?',r'Use output definition::Y=g(state)::The external input is absent from the Moore output function.~Hold stored state::::With no active edge and no asynchronous state control, the state remains M.~Evaluate output::Y=g(M)=1::Changing input can alter next-state logic, but it does not alter the settled current-state output.', 'No; the logical output remains one while state M is held.','The state decoder receives the same stored code before and after the input change; this conclusion excludes asynchronous reset or other explicitly specified controls.'))

P('1-sequential-state','Mealy',r'''
A Mealy machine produces output from both stored state and current input, so a transition label naturally carries input/output information. Its state still remembers history; allowing a direct input dependence does not turn the whole machine into a combinational circuit. Derive output conditions from the event being recognized and derive next-state conditions from the suffix or history needed afterward. These two functions may differ. The model can report a recognition output during the cycle in which the final input is presented, before the state update, under a specified convention. Since changing input can change output without a clock edge, timing or glitch requirements may motivate registering the output in a physical implementation.
''',
E('fundamental','A Mealy controller has stored armed bit Q and output Y=QX. With Q held at one, let input X change 0→1 before the next edge. Find the output change.',r'Evaluate old input::Y=1\cdot0=0::The armed state alone is insufficient to assert output.~Evaluate new input::Y=1\cdot1=1::The current input participates directly in the output function.~Separate state timing::::Q need not change for Y to change; the transition occurs after combinational propagation delay.', 'The output changes from zero to one while the stored state remains armed.','Holding Q=0 would keep Y=0 for either input, demonstrating that both current input and remembered state affect this Mealy output.'),
E('application','Derive a two-state Mealy detector for pattern 10. Let Q=1 mean the previous bit was 1, reset Q=0, and allow overlap. Find equations and outputs for inputs 11010.',r'Derive recognition::Y=Q\bar X::A previous one followed by a current zero completes 10.~Retain needed history::Q^+=X::Remember whether the current bit is one for the next input.~Trace old-state outputs::::For 11010, (old Q,X) are (0,1),(1,1),(1,0),(0,1),(1,0), producing 0,0,1,0,1.', 'Q⁺=X and Y=Q¬X; output ones occur at positions 3 and 5.','The input pairs ending at positions 3 and 5 are exactly 10; no other adjacent pair matches, independently confirming the trace.'),
E('trap','For the Mealy 10 detector, can state Q=1 simply be assigned output one as a Moore label without changing behavior?',r'Compare inputs in the same state::Q=1,X=0\Rightarrow Y=1,\quad Q=1,X=1\Rightarrow Y=0::The two Mealy outputs differ despite identical stored state.~Apply Moore restriction::::A single Moore state can have only one output value.~Show the faulty label::::Assigning output one to Q=1 falsely signals recognition when the current input is another one.', 'No; a behavior-preserving Moore conversion must change state representation or output timing.','The input continuation 11 is a direct counterexample to an always-one label on the previous-one state.'))

P('1-representation-fixed','Number representation',r'''
A bit pattern has no signedness without a representation rule. Unsigned decoding adds positive positional weights; two’s-complement decoding subtracts 2ⁿ when an n-bit code has its sign bit set. Widening a signed value repeats the sign bit because the new negative high-order weight is balanced by the inserted ones. Widening an unsigned value inserts zeros instead. The most negative two’s-complement value is asymmetric: its positive magnitude is outside the same-width signed range. State width at every encode, negate, compare and extend operation. Verify conversions by reconstructing the numeric value, not merely by recognizing a familiar-looking sequence of bits.
''',
E('fundamental','Encode −13 in eight-bit two’s complement.',r'Encode positive magnitude::13=00001101_2::Use the target width before negating.~Invert all eight bits::\overline{00001101}=11110010::Bitwise inversion alone gives one less than the desired two’s-complement code.~Add one::11110010+1=11110011::The low eight bits encode the negative value.', '−13 is 11110011.','Unsigned code 243 minus 256 gives −13; adding code 13 wraps to zero modulo 256.'),
E('application','Interpret eight-bit 10010110 as signed, then widen it to sixteen bits. Compare with zero extension.',r'Decode signed value::150-256=-106::The sign bit is one, so unsigned decoding must be adjusted.~Sign-extend::10010110\to1111111110010110::Repeating the sign bit preserves the signed value.~Contrast zero-extension::0000000010010110=150::Zero extension preserves the unsigned interpretation instead.', 'Signed widening gives 1111111110010110 (0xFF96), still −106; zero extension gives +150.','The signed sixteen-bit code is 65430, and 65430−65536=−106, while the zero-extended code is 150.'),
E('trap','Negate signed eight-bit 10000000 using invert-and-add-one. Is the mathematical positive magnitude representable?',r'Interpret the starting code::10000000=-128::The signed range is −128 through 127.~Apply fixed-width negation::01111111+1=10000000::The bit operation wraps back to the same pattern.~Check mathematical target::+128>127::The desired positive value cannot be encoded at this width.', 'The bit result remains 10000000, and signed negation overflows.','The asymmetric signed endpoints explain why this single nonzero code equals its own modular negation.'))

P('1-representation-fixed','Fixed point',r'''
The binary point is an interpretation attached to an integer code, not necessarily a bit stored alongside it. Increasing fractional precision reduces the step size but narrows the value range at fixed total width. Operations combining different formats must align their scales before addition, comparison or assignment. Conversion to fewer fractional bits discards information and therefore needs a specified rounding rule, especially for negative values. An arithmetic right shift usually rounds negative integers downward, while division truncated toward zero can yield a different result. Treat the destination format’s signed integer range and scale separately: a perfectly aligned computation can still overflow its final storage width.
''',
E('fundamental','An unsigned code 00101101 has three fractional bits. Find its value and the spacing between adjacent codes.',r'Decode integer code::I=45::The bit pattern first represents an ordinary unsigned integer.~Apply implicit scale::x=45/2^3=5.625::Three fractional positions give a denominator of eight.~Find one-code increment::\Delta=1/8=0.125::Changing the stored integer by one changes the value by one scale unit.', 'Value=5.625; spacing=0.125.','Binary interpretation 00101.101 equals 5+1/2+1/8=5.625.'),
E('application','Add fixed-point code 6 with two fractional bits to code 20 with four fractional bits, storing the result with four fractional bits in signed eight-bit storage.',r'Decode input scales::6/4=1.5,\quad20/16=1.25::The numeric codes cannot be added directly because their units differ.~Align to finer scale::6\cdot2^{4-2}=24::Code 24 at scale 1/16 represents the first input exactly.~Add and check::I_{out}=24+20=44,\quad x=44/16=2.75::The code fits signed eight-bit storage.', 'Output code 44 (00101100), representing 2.75.','Adding the decoded values gives 1.5+1.25=2.75; directly adding unaligned codes would incorrectly give 26/16=1.625.'),
E('trap','Convert signed code −3 with two fractional bits to one fractional bit, rounding toward zero. Is an arithmetic right shift by one the correct operation?',r'Find exact destination code::(-3/4)\cdot2=-1.5::The value −0.75 lies halfway between destination codes −2 and −1.~Apply specified rounding::trunc(-1.5)=-1::Rounding toward zero chooses value −0.5.~Compare arithmetic shift::-3\mathbin{\mathrm{ashr}}1=-2::The shift rounds downward and instead gives destination value −1.', 'No; round-toward-zero requires code −1, while arithmetic shifting yields −2.','The respective value errors are +0.25 and −0.25; the direction of the error reveals the different rounding conventions.'))

P('1-representation-floating','Floating point',r'''
Floating-point spacing depends on magnitude because the exponent scales a finite-precision significand. Normalization chooses a standard leading bit, while reserved exponent patterns encode zero, subnormals, infinities and NaNs rather than ordinary normalized values. To encode a finite normal number, write it in binary, move the point to follow the first one, store a biased exponent and retain the specified fraction bits. Arithmetic must align exponents and round the significand afterward. An exact real answer may lie halfway between representable numbers, so the tie rule matters. Specify precision as total significand bits or stored fraction bits explicitly; the implicit leading one makes those counts differ for normal values.
''',
E('fundamental','Encode positive 6.5 in IEEE single precision, giving sign, biased exponent and leading fraction bits.',r'Convert and normalize::6.5=110.1_2=1.101_2\cdot2^2::Normalization separates significand and exponent.~Encode sign and exponent::s=0,\quad E=2+127=129=10000001_2::The stored exponent includes the bias.~Store the fraction::f=10100000000000000000000::The normalized leading one is implicit rather than stored.', 'Fields are 0 | 10000001 | 10100000000000000000000, hexadecimal 0x40D00000.','Decoding gives (1+1/2+1/8)×4=1.625×4=6.5.'),
E('application','A binary floating format retains three total significand bits and rounds to nearest, ties to even. Add 1.00×2³ and 1.00×2⁰.',r'Align exponents::1.00\cdot2^0=0.00100\cdot2^3::Both operands need the same exponent before addition.~Form exact sum::1.00100\cdot2^3=9::The exact significand requires a bit beyond the three-bit precision.~Resolve the tie::::Representable neighbors are 1.00×2³=8 and 1.01×2³=10. The retained low bit of 1.00 is even, so the tie rounds to 8.', 'The rounded sum is 8 even though the exact sum is 9.','The two representable neighbors are each one unit from 9; the even retained-bit rule chooses the lower neighbor in this particular tie.'),
E('trap','An IEEE single-precision code has sign 1, exponent field zero and fraction zero. May the normal formula with hidden leading one be applied?',r'Identify reserved exponent::::An all-zero exponent is outside the normal-number field rule.~Inspect zero fraction::::With exponent and fraction both zero, the encoding represents zero; the sign distinguishes its signed code.~Apply special interpretation::x=-0::There is no implicit leading one in this zero encoding.', 'It represents negative zero; the normal hidden-one formula is invalid.','Applying the normal formula would invent a nonzero negative value. Under ordinary IEEE equality, −0 compares equal to +0 despite distinct sign bits.'))

P('1-representation-fixed-multiply','Arithmetic',r'''
Hardware arithmetic produces a fixed-width bit result whose numeric interpretation must then be checked. Unsigned operations follow arithmetic modulo 2ⁿ when high bits are discarded; signed operations use the same low bits but have a different representability test. Subtraction can reuse addition by complementing the subtrahend and adding one, provided all operands use the same width. Multiplication needs more result bits before truncation or fixed-point rescaling, because a narrow intermediate can destroy information that the final format would need. Interpret carries, borrows, signed overflow and rounding as separate outcomes. A useful check compares the decoded bit answer with exact arithmetic and explains any mismatch using the stated storage convention.
''',
E('fundamental','Compute −3−4 with a four-bit two’s-complement adder.',r'Encode at common width::A=1101,\quad B=0100::These codes represent −3 and +4.~Prepare subtraction::\bar B+1=1011+1=1100::Adding the two’s-complement negation of B implements A−B.~Add and retain::1101+1100=11001\Rightarrow1001::The low four bits represent −7, which fits the signed range.', 'Result=1001, representing −7, with no signed overflow.','Exact arithmetic gives −3−4=−7; decoding code 9 as signed gives 9−16=−7.'),
E('application','Multiply unsigned four-bit values 12 and 3. Find the full product and the result if only four low bits are retained.',r'Compute exact product::12\cdot3=36::An eight-bit intermediate can represent any product of two four-bit unsigned operands.~Encode the widened result::36=00100100_2::The full product retains both high and low parts.~Apply four-bit storage::36\bmod16=4=0100_2::Discarding high bits changes the represented mathematical value.', 'The full product is 00100100; the retained four-bit result is 0100 (4), with unsigned overflow.','12×(2+1)=24+12=36; the retained code satisfies 36=2×16+4.'),
E('trap','Add signed four-bit 0101 and 0100. The carry-out is zero; can that prove no overflow?',r'Interpret operands::5+4=9::The exact sum exceeds the four-bit signed maximum 7.~Compute bit result::0101+0100=1001::The retained signed code is −7 despite positive inputs.~Check sign condition::::Two positive operands produced a negative result, proving signed overflow even without final carry.', 'No; signed overflow occurs although carry-out is zero.','Carry into the sign bit is one and carry out is zero, so their XOR is one, agreeing with the sign-based test.'))

P('2-instructions-isa','Instruction set',r'''
An ISA defines the software-visible contract: instruction encodings, operand interpretation, register effects and exceptional behavior. A microarchitecture chooses how to implement that contract and can change pipeline depth or CPI without changing what a valid instruction means. Encoding questions are allocation problems: identify the bits available for opcodes and operands, count patterns, then account for reserved formats. Expanding opcodes reuse space from instructions needing fewer operands, but a prefix already assigned to a longer-operand instruction cannot be reused ambiguously. Signed immediate ranges depend on the specified field interpretation, not on total instruction width. Verify an allocation by tracing how a decoder distinguishes every instruction format before examining its remaining operand bits.
''',
E('fundamental','A sixteen-bit instruction has a five-bit opcode, a three-bit register field and an eight-bit signed two’s-complement immediate. Find the field capacities.',r'Check total width::5+3+8=16::All instruction bits are accounted for.~Count opcode and register names::N_{op}=2^5=32,\quad N_{reg}=2^3=8::Each binary field names one pattern per bit combination.~Interpret immediate range::-2^7\le imm\le2^7-1::The sign bit gives a range of −128 through 127 rather than 0 through 255.', 'At most 32 opcode patterns and 8 register names; signed immediates range from −128 to 127.','The three field spaces contain 32×8×256=65536 patterns, exactly 2¹⁶ before any reserved encodings.'),
E('application','A sixteen-bit ISA uses six-bit operands. Twelve two-operand opcodes use twelve of the sixteen high-four-bit prefixes. Use expanding opcodes to support 200 one-operand instructions; how many zero-operand instructions can remain?',r'Count available one-operand opcodes::(16-12)2^6=256::Each free high-four-bit prefix can extend through six additional opcode bits while retaining one six-bit operand.~Reserve one-operand patterns::256-200=56::These unassigned ten-bit prefixes can be extended further.~Expand zero-operand encodings::56\cdot2^6=3584::With no operand field, all remaining low-six-bit patterns become opcode extensions.', 'At most 3584 zero-operand instructions remain under this allocation.','Two-operand instructions consume 12×4096 patterns and one-operand instructions consume 200×64; subtracting both from 65536 leaves 3584.'),
E('trap','Two processors implement the same ISA. One uses microcoded execution and the other a deeper pipeline. Must a valid ADD instruction produce different arithmetic results because their CPI differs?',r'Identify the architectural contract::::The ISA specifies operands, result width and visible effects of ADD.~Separate implementation timing::::Microsteps and pipeline overlap change how long the operation takes, not its specified arithmetic semantics.~Apply identical input state::::Given the same architectural input state and specified conditions, both implementations must produce the same ADD result.', 'No; CPI can differ while ISA-visible arithmetic behavior remains identical.','A difference in the result for a valid, fully specified ADD would indicate an implementation error or a different ISA contract, not merely a performance difference.'))

P('2-instructions-isa','Addressing modes',r'''
An addressing mode is a recipe for obtaining an operand, and not every recipe produces a memory address. Immediate mode supplies a constant; register mode supplies a register value; memory modes first compute an effective address and then access its contents. Indirection introduces an additional address lookup, while scaled indexing combines a base with an index expressed in elements. PC-relative addressing requires a declared reference PC and displacement unit. Keep a small table of intermediate values to avoid confusing an address with data stored there. Negative offsets must be sign-extended according to their field width, and byte-addressed versus word-addressed conventions can change a target even when the encoded numbers look identical.
''',
E('fundamental','A PC-relative branch adds a signed byte displacement −8 to the next-instruction PC, which is 1004. Find its target.',r'Choose the defined reference::PC_{ref}=1004::The instruction specifies next-instruction PC rather than the branch’s own address.~Interpret displacement units::d=-8\text{ bytes}::No instruction-size scaling is applied because the offset is already in bytes.~Compute target::EA=1004-8=996::The signed displacement points backward.', 'The branch target is byte address 996.','Moving eight bytes forward from 996 returns the specified reference 1004; using a different PC would violate the stated ISA convention.'),
E('application','A byte-addressed load uses base 2000, element index 7 scaled by 4 bytes, and displacement 12. Memory at the effective address contains 42. Find address and loaded value.',r'Scale the element index::offset=7\cdot4=28\text{ bytes}::The register index counts elements rather than bytes.~Add base and displacement::EA=2000+28+12=2040::All combined quantities now use byte-address units.~Fetch contents::operand=memory[2040]=42::The load obtains the value stored at that address.', 'Effective address=2040; loaded operand=42.','The address difference 2040−2000 equals seven four-byte elements plus twelve bytes; 2040 and 42 serve different roles.'),
E('trap','A memory-indirect operand is defined as memory[memory[100]]. Given memory[100]=200 and memory[200]=37, is the operand 200?',r'Perform the address lookup::EA=memory[100]=200::The first memory value supplies the effective address.~Perform the operand lookup::operand=memory[EA]=memory[200]=37::The mode requires a second access for actual data.~Classify the mistaken answer::::Returning 200 stops after address indirection and omits the required data fetch.', 'The operand is 37; 200 is its effective address.','Replacing the inner memory expression by its supplied value gives memory[200], whose contents are independently specified as 37.'))

P('2-instructions-performance','CPU performance',r'''
Execution time is a chain of units: executed instructions become cycles through average CPI, then cycles become seconds through clock rate. Dynamic instruction frequencies determine the weights in average CPI; counting source lines or distinct opcodes gives different data. Speedup compares complete old and new elapsed times, including unchanged work. When only one fraction improves, express its new contribution as original time divided by the improvement factor and leave the other contribution alone. Rates should be combined according to the workload: sequential jobs require adding their times before computing total throughput. State whether memory stalls are already included in CPI to avoid adding the same penalty twice.
''',
E('fundamental','A program executes two million instructions: 40% have CPI 1 and 60% CPI 3. The clock is 2 GHz. Find average CPI and CPU time.',r'Weight by dynamic fractions::CPI=0.4(1)+0.6(3)=2.2::The average reflects how often each class executes.~Count cycles::cycles=2\cdot10^6(2.2)=4.4\cdot10^6::Multiply executed instructions by their average cycle cost.~Convert cycles to time::T=4.4\cdot10^6/(2\cdot10^9)=2.2\ ms::The clock supplies two billion cycles per second.', 'Average CPI=2.2; execution time=2.2 ms.','Class counts give 0.8 million one-cycle instructions plus 1.2 million three-cycle instructions, totaling 4.4 million cycles.'),
E('application','A change accelerates the part responsible for 80% of a program’s original time by a factor of four. Find overall speedup.',r'Normalize original time::T_{old}=1::The accelerated and unchanged contributions are 0.8 and 0.2.~Compute new contributions::T_{new}=0.8/4+0.2=0.4::Only the accelerated part is divided by four.~Take time ratio::speedup=1/0.4=2.5::Overall performance is limited by unchanged work.', 'Overall speedup is 2.5, not four.','An original 100-second run becomes 20 seconds for improved work plus 20 unchanged seconds, giving 40 seconds and the same ratio.'),
E('trap','Two equal-sized jobs run sequentially, one in 1 second and the other in 3 seconds. Is overall throughput the arithmetic mean of 1 and 1/3 jobs per second?',r'Add elapsed times::T=1+3=4\text{ seconds}::Both jobs must finish in sequence.~Count completed work::N=2\text{ jobs}::The numerator covers the same complete workload.~Compute total rate::R=N/T=2/4=0.5\text{ jobs/s}::The arithmetic mean of per-job rates incorrectly gives equal time weight to unequal durations.', 'Overall throughput is 0.5 jobs per second.','A repeated pair completes four jobs in eight seconds, again giving 0.5 rather than the arithmetic rate mean 2/3.'))

P('2-alu-control-alu','Design of ALU',r'''
An ALU combines candidate arithmetic and logic results with control decoding that selects one operation. Subtraction commonly shares an adder by XORing the second operand with a subtract-control bit and using that bit as carry-in. Logical operations apply independently at each bit, whereas arithmetic operations couple positions through carries. Flags must be derived under the chosen operation and number interpretation: negative reports the sign bit, zero tests the entire result, and carry or borrow conventions are architecture-specific. Shift hardware also needs a distinction between arithmetic and logical right shifts. Verify both the selected result and its flags; checking a correct low-bit result alone does not validate the surrounding control or status logic.
''',
E('fundamental','For eight-bit operands A=10101010 and B=11001100, compute the ALU’s bitwise AND and XOR results.',r'Apply AND columnwise::A\land B=10001000::A result bit is one only where both corresponding input bits are one.~Apply XOR columnwise::A\oplus B=01100110::A result bit is one where exactly one input bit is one.~Interpret operation control::::Selecting AND or XOR chooses different combinational result paths for the same operands.', 'AND=10001000 (0x88); XOR=01100110 (0x66).','Hexadecimal checking gives 0xAA AND 0xCC=0x88 and 0xAA XOR 0xCC=0x66.'),
E('application','An eight-bit ALU subtracts 9 from 6 using A+¬B+1. Carry-out means no unsigned borrow. Find result, carry, zero and negative flags.',r'Prepare subtract input::A=00000110,\quad\bar B=11110110::Complement the eight-bit subtrahend 00001001.~Add with carry-in::6+246+1=253=11111101_2::The sum is below 256, so there is no ninth-bit carry.~Set defined flags::C=0,\quad Z=0,\quad N=1::The result is nonzero with its sign bit set; zero carry means an unsigned borrow under the stated convention.', 'Result=11111101 (signed −3); C=0, Z=0, N=1, and no signed overflow.','6−9=−3 lies in the signed range; unsigned subtraction needs a borrow, agreeing with the specified no-borrow carry flag.'),
E('trap','Right-shift eight-bit 10000000 by one. Can an arithmetic shift and a logical shift be used interchangeably for signed values?',r'Apply logical shift::10000000\to01000000::Zero enters the high bit, producing unsigned/signed value 64.~Apply arithmetic shift::10000000\to11000000::The sign bit is replicated, producing signed value −64.~Compare signed intent::-128/2=-64::Only the arithmetic shift preserves the signed division interpretation in this exact even case.', 'No; logical shifting gives +64, while arithmetic shifting gives −64.','Signed decoding of 11000000 is 192−256=−64; decoding 01000000 is +64.'))

P('2-alu-control-hardwired-decode','Hardwired control',r'''
Hardwired control turns state and instruction conditions into Boolean control and next-state signals. Outputs answer which datapath actions happen now; next-state equations answer which execution phase follows at the next clock edge. Derive these separately because a state may assert the same output under several conditions while branching differently afterward. Binary state encodings require decoding, whereas one-hot state bits can directly select phases; changing encoding changes hardware equations but not the intended sequence. Reset and illegal states also require explicit policies. A control table is an implementation specification, not just a list of mnemonics. Verify every guarded action using both the state code and its opcode or readiness condition.
''',
E('fundamental','A binary controller asserts RegWrite only in state q₁q₀=10 when opcode condition ADD=1. Derive RegWrite and evaluate state 10 with ADD=0.',r'Decode execution state::E=q_1\bar q_0::The state product selects code 10 exactly.~Include instruction condition::RegWrite=q_1\bar q_0\cdot ADD::Entering the state alone is insufficient unless the correct opcode is active.~Evaluate excluded opcode::q_1q_0=10,\ ADD=0\Rightarrow RegWrite=0::The guard blocks an unintended register update.', 'RegWrite=q₁¬q₀·ADD; it is zero for the supplied non-ADD case.','Substitution at all other state codes also yields zero; only the specified state/opcode combination enables writing.'),
E('application','An idle/busy hardwired controller uses Q=0 for idle and Q=1 for busy. Start begins work only while idle; Done ends it only while busy. Derive Q⁺ and trace (Start,Done)=(1,0),(0,0),(0,1) from idle.',r'Derive idle branch::Q=0\Rightarrow Q^+=Start::A start request enters busy state.~Derive busy branch::Q=1\Rightarrow Q^+=\overline{Done}::Busy remains asserted until completion.~Combine and trace::Q^+=\bar QStart+Q\overline{Done}::The three sampled pairs produce states 1,1,0.', 'Q⁺=¬Q·Start+Q·¬Done; the state trace is busy,busy,idle.','The middle step ignores Start while busy and holds until Done; the last step consumes completion rather than waiting for a new start.'),
E('trap','A controller uses only gates and state flip-flops. Can editing program RAM rewrite its next-state function, so the same encoded state and opcode now produce a different next state?',r'Locate transition implementation::::Next-state and control-output functions reside in logic gates, not in a fetched microinstruction table.~Separate program input from control logic::::New instruction bytes can select already-supported opcode cases but do not rewrite the controller’s Boolean equations.~Identify an actual modification::::Changing the execution sequencing rules requires changing the hardware logic or a separately specified programmable mechanism.', 'No; program RAM contents do not themselves rewrite a purely hardwired control unit.','The same state and decoded-input combination still feeds the same gates after the RAM edit, so its prescribed transition remains unchanged.'))

P('2-alu-control-control','Microprogrammed control',r'''
Microprogrammed control represents sequencing as words in a control store. Each word selects datapath actions and either names or helps compute the next microinstruction address. A sequence must respect data dependencies and resource conflicts even when a wide horizontal word can assert many control bits. Encoding mutually exclusive choices compresses a vertical word but requires decoding before driving the datapath. Account separately for control fields, branch-condition fields and next-address fields when calculating width. The architectural instruction count is not the microinstruction count, and a memory operation may require waiting under the stated device timing. Verify a microsequence by following the values available at each step rather than assuming all register transfers can occur together.
''',
E('fundamental','A simple load microsequence uses μ₀: MAR←Rbase; μ₁: MDR←memory[MAR]; μ₂: Rdest←MDR. Rbase=120 and memory[120]=77; each memory read completes within its stated microstep. Trace the result.',r'Address setup::::At μ₀, MAR receives the base register value 120, selecting the memory location.~Memory transfer::::At μ₁, the completed read places memory[120]=77 into MDR.~Destination transfer::::At μ₂, the destination register receives MDR=77; the architectural load is now complete.', 'The destination register becomes 77 after three specified microsteps.','Substituting the register values along the microsequence gives Rdest=memory[Rbase]=memory[120]=77, while the address 120 remains separate from the data.'),
E('application','A control word chooses one of 6 ALU operations, 4 bus sources and 8 destinations, plus a six-bit next address. Compare encoded fields with separate one-hot fields for these groups over 64 words.',r'Calculate encoded controls::b=\lceil\log_2 6\rceil+\log_2 4+\log_2 8=3+2+3=8::Each group is mutually exclusive, permitting binary encoding.~Include next address::W_{encoded}=8+6=14,\quad W_{onehot}=6+4+8+6=24::Both designs retain the same sequencer field.~Count storage::64(14)/8=112\ bytes,\quad64(24)/8=192\ bytes::The example counts only control-store bits, allowing packed storage.', 'Encoded storage=112 bytes; one-hot field storage=192 bytes under the supplied format.','The difference is 64×10=640 bits=80 bytes; encoded patterns outside the six ALU choices need a defined invalid-code policy.'),
E('trap','A horizontal microinstruction on a single shared bus asserts both R1out and R2out, where R1=5 and R2=9. Does its wide word make this simultaneous transfer valid?',r'Inspect the physical resource::::Both output controls enable different registers to drive the same bus.~Compare driven values::R1\ne R2::The drivers request incompatible bit values on at least some bus lines.~Repair the microsequence::::Select only one source per microstep or provide a datapath with independent buses; word width cannot create a second bus.', 'No; this word causes a bus-source conflict in the stated datapath.','A destination has no defined single bus value when conflicting drivers are simultaneously active, so the transfer cannot be validated as an ordinary register assignment.'))

P('2-cache-two-level-hierarchy','Memory hierarchy',r'''
The purpose of a hierarchy is to exploit locality while limiting expensive lower-level accesses. Temporal locality concerns reuse of the same item; spatial locality concerns nearby items brought in together by a block transfer. Trace which level sees each request before applying a miss rate. Global and local rates share a numerator but use different denominators, so their relationship follows directly from traffic counts. Inclusion is a separate policy: an inclusive lower cache guarantees that upper-cache blocks also appear below, and a lower-level eviction may therefore require an upper-level invalidation. Do not assume inclusion, equal block sizes or free writebacks unless the problem explicitly supplies those conditions.
''',
E('fundamental','An initially empty cache fetches aligned 16-byte blocks. Read byte addresses 0,4,8,12 with no intervening eviction. Explain the hierarchy benefit.',r'Identify the common block::\lfloor0/16\rfloor=\lfloor4/16\rfloor=\lfloor8/16\rfloor=\lfloor12/16\rfloor=0::All four addresses belong to one transferred block.~Fetch on first request::::The access to address 0 misses and brings bytes 0–15 from the lower level.~Reuse neighboring data::::The remaining three accesses hit, exploiting spatial locality rather than requiring three more lower-level transfers.', 'One block fetch serves all four references: miss,hit,hit,hit.','The number of lower-level reads is one although four different byte addresses are requested, directly illustrating the block-transfer advantage.'),
E('application','An L1 cache misses on 20% of processor references. L2 misses on 5% of all processor references. Find L2’s local miss rate.',r'Choose a reference count::N_{CPU}=1000\Rightarrow N_{L2}=200::Only L1 misses reach L2.~Count L2 misses globally::N_{L2miss}=0.05(1000)=50::The supplied five-percent rate uses all processor references as denominator.~Condition on reaching L2::m_{L2,local}=50/200=0.25::Local rate divides by actual L2 requests.', 'The L2 local miss rate is 25%.','Multiplying L1’s 0.20 miss fraction by local L2 rate 0.25 recovers the given global miss fraction 0.05.'),
E('trap','In an inclusive L1/L2 hierarchy with equal block size, may L2 evict block X while leaving a valid copy of X in L1?',r'Apply inclusion invariant::::Every valid L1 block must also be present in L2.~Consider the proposed eviction::::Removing X only from L2 would leave an L1 block absent from the required lower-level superset.~Maintain the invariant::::The hierarchy must invalidate the upper copy or use another specified inclusion-preserving action when X is evicted below.', 'No; leaving the L1 copy valid would break the stated inclusive policy.','After the upper invalidation, the set of valid L1 blocks is again a subset of L2’s blocks; an exclusive hierarchy would have a different contract.'))

P('2-cache-mapping','Cache mapping',r'''
Cache mapping limits the candidate locations for a memory block, while replacement chooses a victim within those candidates. Derive the block number before considering placement, since byte offset bits do not participate in tag matching. Set count depends on data capacity, block size and associativity; increasing ways at fixed capacity reduces index bits and increases tag bits. Trace replacement order within each set independently. A miss on the first reference to a block is compulsory even if its insertion evicts another block. To distinguish later conflict misses from capacity misses, compare against a fully associative cache with the same capacity and replacement assumption, rather than treating every same-set eviction as the same miss category.
''',
E('fundamental','A byte-addressed 32-bit system has a 4 KiB, two-way cache with 16-byte blocks. Find offset, index and tag widths; capacity counts data only.',r'Compute set count::S=4096/(16\cdot2)=128::There are two data lines per set.~Allocate offset and index::b_{offset}=\log_2 16=4,\quad b_{index}=\log_2 128=7::Low bits choose bytes and sets.~Allocate remaining bits::b_{tag}=32-4-7=21::Tag bits distinguish blocks mapping to the same set.', 'Offset=4 bits, index=7 bits, tag=21 bits.','128 sets×2 ways×16 bytes=4096 bytes, and the three address fields sum to 32 bits.'),
E('application','An initially empty cache has two sets and two ways per set, using LRU. Trace block numbers 0,2,4,0 and classify the final miss against an equal-capacity fully associative LRU cache.',r'Map blocks::0\bmod2=2\bmod2=4\bmod2=0::All three distinct blocks compete in set zero.~Trace set-local LRU::::After 0 and 2, the set holds both, with 0 older. Bringing in 4 evicts 0; returning to 0 therefore misses.~Compare equal capacity::::The fully associative four-line cache retains all three distinct blocks, so its final access to 0 hits.', 'The trace is miss,miss,miss,miss; the final miss is a conflict miss.','The three first references are compulsory. Three blocks fit in the four-line total capacity, so the final miss comes from placement restriction rather than total capacity.'),
E('trap','A fully associative cache uses 64-byte blocks in a 32-bit byte-addressed system. Does having no set index mean it also needs no tag?',r'Allocate block offset::b_{offset}=\log_2 64=6::Six low bits identify a byte within a block.~Remove only the index field::b_{index}=0::Any cache line may hold any block, so no address bits select a set.~Retain block identity::b_{tag}=32-6=26::Tags still distinguish which of the many memory blocks each line holds.', 'It needs 26 tag bits per stored block identity, despite having zero index bits.','Addresses 0 and 64 share offset zero but name different blocks; tags must distinguish them when placement alone does not.'))

P('2-cache-hierarchy','Cache performance',r'''
A cache performance calculation is a weighted account of costs and events. Decide whether miss time includes the hit lookup or is an extra penalty, then use one consistent convention. If cache stalls are converted into CPI, count memory references per instruction and multiply by the miss fraction and penalty in cycles. Instructions and data accesses can contribute separate streams, and writes may have additional policy-dependent costs. Improving one number, such as hit latency, can worsen overall time if misses become more common. Validate a result using a finite reference population or a complete-path weighted sum, and keep cycle penalties separate from nanosecond penalties until a clock period explicitly connects the units.
''',
E('fundamental','Hits take a complete 2 ns and misses a complete 42 ns. The hit rate is 90%. Find average access time.',r'Use mutually exclusive paths::AMAT=hT_{hit}+(1-h)T_{miss}::The supplied miss time already includes the whole miss path.~Weight each path::AMAT=0.9(2)+0.1(42)::Ninety percent pay the hit path and ten percent the complete miss path.~Add costs::AMAT=1.8+4.2=6\ ns::No additional baseline is added afterward.', 'Average access time is 6 ns.','The equivalent incremental penalty is 42−2=40 ns, giving 2+0.1×40=6 ns.'),
E('application','Base CPI excludes memory stalls and equals 1. Each instruction fetches once; 25% also access data once. All accesses miss at rate 4%, with an extra 40-cycle penalty and no overlap. Find effective CPI.',r'Count references per instruction::R=1+0.25=1.25::Instruction fetches occur for all instructions and data accesses for the stated fraction.~Compute expected stall cycles::\Delta CPI=1.25(0.04)(40)=2::Every expected miss adds its nonoverlapped penalty.~Add baseline::CPI_{effective}=1+2=3::The base cost and extra memory stalls are disjoint by specification.', 'Effective CPI is 3.','For 100 instructions there are 125 references, five expected misses and 200 extra cycles; adding 100 base cycles gives 300 total.'),
E('trap','Cache A has hit time 2 ns, miss rate 2%, extra penalty 50 ns. Cache B lowers hit time to 1 ns but misses 10%, with the same penalty. Is B faster on average?',r'Calculate original average::AMAT_A=2+0.02(50)=3\ ns::The small miss fraction adds only one nanosecond.~Calculate redesigned average::AMAT_B=1+0.10(50)=6\ ns::The faster hit is outweighed by expected miss cost.~Compare actual request time::AMAT_B/AMAT_A=2::The redesigned cache doubles average access time under this workload.', 'No; B averages 6 ns versus A’s 3 ns.','The one-nanosecond hit saving is smaller than the four-nanosecond increase in expected penalty, leaving a net three-nanosecond regression.'))

P('2-memory-io-chips','Memory interfacing',r'''
A memory chip’s depth and word width are independent dimensions. Parallel chips widen each addressed word, while banks selected by higher address bits increase the number of locations. Identify whether the CPU addresses bytes or complete words before assigning address pins: byte lanes can use low CPU address bits that never reach the chip’s word-address input. Chip-enable decoding should choose a bank without enabling competing drivers on the same data lane. Partial decoding can cause aliases, where multiple CPU addresses reach the same physical location. Verify an arrangement by multiplying chip count by per-chip bit capacity and by tracing one complete CPU address into bank, chip location and lane selection.
''',
E('fundamental','A chip is specified as 4K×8, with K=1024 and word addressing. Find address-input count and byte capacity.',r'Count locations::D=4\cdot1024=4096::Depth is the number of independently addressed words.~Find address pins::a=\log_2 4096=12::Twelve binary inputs select one chip word.~Convert capacity::4096\cdot8/8=4096\text{ bytes}::Each word is exactly one byte wide.', 'Twelve address inputs and 4 KiB of stored data.','2¹² distinct word addresses each select eight data bits, totaling 32768 bits.'),
E('application','Construct 16K×32 memory from 4K×8 chips. Find chip count, bank selection and the CPU byte-address width if every byte is addressable.',r'Expand width and depth::N_{width}=32/8=4,\quad N_{banks}=16K/4K=4::Each bank uses four parallel chips, and four banks supply depth.~Count chips and word addresses::N=4\cdot4=16,\quad a_{word}=12+2=14::Twelve bits address within a chip and two select a bank.~Account for byte lanes::capacity=16K\cdot4=64\ KiB,\quad a_{byte}=14+2=16::Two low byte-address bits identify a lane within each 32-bit word.', 'Sixteen chips; two bank bits and twelve within-chip address bits; sixteen CPU byte-address bits overall.','16×4096×8 bits equals 16384×32 bits. Four bytes per word explain the extra two CPU address bits rather than two extra chip depth bits.'),
E('trap','Eight 1K×1 chips share address lines and drive separate data-bit lanes. Does this arrangement create 8K addressable words?',r'Identify connection type::::Common address inputs with separate data lanes widen the selected word rather than creating more addresses.~Count locations::D=1024::All eight chips select the same numbered location simultaneously.~Count resulting width::W=8\cdot1=8::Together they provide one byte at each of the existing 1024 addresses.', 'The arrangement is 1K×8, not 8K×1 or 8K words.','There are still only ten address bits, yielding 1024 addresses; total bit capacity is 8192, but bit count is not address count.'))

P('2-memory-io-io-register-trace','I/O interface',r'''
An I/O register defines an interaction with a device, including any side effects of reads and writes. The same load/store instruction used for RAM may therefore have a very different meaning at a device address. Read-modify-write is safe only when the register’s semantics permit reading and rewriting unchanged bits. A write-one-to-clear status register illustrates the danger: writing an old one back actively clears that event. Decode register offsets and transfer widths carefully, and preserve alignment when the interface requires full words. Describe which values are addresses, which are control masks and which are returned data. Correct interface use follows its register contract, not a general assumption that every mapped address behaves like ordinary storage.
''',
E('fundamental','An ordinary read/write control register is initially 0x02. Set enable bit 3 while preserving other bits; reads have no side effects. Find the written value.',r'Build enable mask::mask=1\ll3=0x08::Bit numbering starts at zero.~Read current control value::::The supplied value 0x02 already has bit 1 set, which must be preserved.~Merge requested bit::new=0x02\mathbin{\mathrm{OR}}0x08=0x0A::OR sets bit 3 without clearing other set bits.', 'Write 0x0A to the control register.','Binary 00000010 becomes 00001010, changing only the requested enable bit under the stated ordinary read/write semantics.'),
E('application','A 32-bit memory-mapped interface starts at 0x40000000: CTRL is offset 0, STATUS offset 4 and DATA offset 8. A program adds an offset of two to the base to read DATA. Correct the address and explain the width issue.',r'Interpret register offsets::::The interface specifies byte offsets, so DATA is eight bytes from the base.~Calculate actual data address::EA=0x40000000+8=0x40000008::This is aligned to a four-byte word.~Diagnose the shortcut::::Adding two directly gives byte address 0x40000002, which is not DATA. Two register positions must be scaled by four bytes.', 'Read DATA at 0x40000008, using the interface’s specified 32-bit transfer width.','Register-number two multiplied by four-byte spacing yields offset eight; address low bits 00 confirm full-word alignment.'),
E('trap','A write-one-to-clear STATUS register currently reads 1010. Clear only bit 1. Is writing the read value 1010 back safe?',r'Apply register semantics::::A written one clears its corresponding pending bit; zero leaves that bit unchanged.~Construct the requested mask::write=0010::Only the requested bit should receive a clearing one.~Compare writeback behavior::::Writing 1010 would clear both bits 3 and 1, losing the separate event at bit 3.', 'Write 0010; ordinary read-modify-write using the whole read value is unsafe here.','Under write-one-to-clear semantics, 1010 AND NOT 0010 gives remaining status 1000, whereas writing 1010 would give 0000.'))

P('2-memory-io-io','Interrupt',r'''
An interrupt is a control transfer that lets a device or event request processor attention. Servicing requires preserving the interrupted architectural context, identifying and acknowledging the source, performing the required work and restoring execution. Entry latency differs from handler duration: masking or a higher-priority handler can delay entry even when the eventual handler is short. Level-triggered requests remain asserted until the source condition is removed, so returning without acknowledgment can immediately retrigger service. An interrupt does not by itself move an entire data block; software or DMA still performs the transfer. State priority, masking and acknowledgment assumptions when computing response time or counting service events.
''',
E('fundamental','An interrupt is accepted between instructions, with saved next PC=200 and handler entry address 128. After a correct return-from-interrupt, where does execution resume?',r'Save the interrupted context::::The next PC=200 identifies the instruction that should execute when service finishes.~Enter the handler::PC=128::The interrupt vector supplies a temporary control-transfer target, not the continuation address.~Restore on return::PC=200::Return-from-interrupt restores the saved continuation and required processor state.', 'Execution resumes at address 200.','The handler’s own last instruction address is unrelated to the saved continuation; restoring 128 would simply reenter the handler.'),
E('application','An interrupt arrives just after interrupts are masked for a maximum 120 μs critical section. Entry overhead is 8 μs, and the first device-service operation occurs 3 μs after entry. Find worst-case time to first service, assuming no other delays.',r'Count masking delay::T_{wait}=120\ \mu s::Arrival at the start of the maximum masked interval is the worst case.~Include context-entry work::T_{entry}=8\ \mu s::Service cannot begin before the processor enters the handler.~Include handler prefix::T_{response}=120+8+3=131\ \mu s::The first service operation follows the specified three-microsecond prefix.', 'Worst-case time to first device service is 131 μs.','An arrival after the masked interval would need only 11 μs; the extra 120 μs is blocking delay rather than handler execution time.'),
E('trap','A level-triggered device keeps its interrupt line high until its pending flag is acknowledged. A handler returns without clearing that flag. Will there necessarily be only one interrupt?',r'Inspect source condition::::The device’s pending flag remains set, so its request line remains high.~Restore acceptance::::After return reenables acceptance, the still-asserted level can again request service.~Remove the cause::::The handler must perform the specified device acknowledgment, not merely return or clear a CPU-local entry indicator.', 'No; the uncleared level can immediately retrigger the handler.','Clearing the pending device flag deasserts the line and prevents repeated service for the same unchanged source condition.'))

P('2-memory-io-io','DMA',r'''
DMA separates processor setup from the controller’s repeated data transfers. The controller needs source or destination addresses, length, direction and a valid ownership/coherence protocol. Trace how addresses advance with each transfer beat and distinguish byte counts from word counts. DMA reduces per-byte software work but still consumes bus or memory bandwidth, so cycle stealing can slow processor memory accesses. In systems without automatic cache coherence, memory updates made by a DMA device may not immediately appear in a processor’s cached copy; cache maintenance or an uncached buffer policy is then part of correct use. Compute CPU overhead and transfer elapsed time separately because they measure different benefits and costs.
''',
E('fundamental','A DMA controller copies 12 bytes from source address 400 to destination 800 using four-byte beats and incrementing addresses. List beat addresses.',r'Count beats::N=12/4=3::The byte count is exactly divisible by the transfer width.~Trace source progression::::The three beats begin at source addresses 400,404 and 408.~Trace destination progression::::The matching destinations are 800,804 and 808, covering destination bytes 800 through 811.', 'Three beats: 400→800, 404→804, 408→808.','Each four-byte beat covers a disjoint range; their union contains exactly twelve source and twelve destination bytes.'),
E('application','A cycle-stealing DMA reserves 20% of bus cycles. A processor workload continuously needs the bus and previously completed at one memory operation per cycle. Ignore endpoint effects and other bottlenecks; find its steady-state slowdown.',r'Allocate remaining bandwidth::f_{CPU}=1-0.20=0.80::The processor can use only the non-DMA fraction.~Relate work to elapsed cycles::T_{new}=T_{old}/0.80::The same operation count now requires more total cycles.~Compute slowdown::T_{new}/T_{old}=1.25::DMA saves CPU copying work elsewhere but constrains this bus-bound workload.', 'The processor workload takes 1.25 times as long in the stated steady-state model.','Across five recurring bus slots, four serve CPU operations and one serves DMA, giving four operations per five cycles rather than one per cycle.'),
E('trap','A noncoherent processor cache holds an old clean copy of a buffer. DMA writes new bytes into main memory. Can the CPU read the buffer immediately and assume it sees the new bytes?',r'Locate the DMA update::::The controller changed main memory, while the noncoherent cache received no automatic update.~Follow a cached CPU read::::A cache hit can return the old resident bytes without consulting main memory.~Apply required protocol::::Use the platform’s cache invalidation/synchronization or a suitable uncached buffer policy before consuming the DMA result.', 'No; a stale cached copy can hide the new DMA data.','Once the clean stale line is invalidated and the read fetches the updated memory block, the CPU can observe the new bytes; dirty-line cases need additional protocol care.'))

P('2-pipeline-overlap','Instruction pipelining',r'''
Pipelining overlaps stages from different instructions; it does not make one instruction skip its stages. Determine the clock from the slowest stage plus pipeline-register overhead, then count fill, successive issues and any inserted bubbles. In a finite sequence, the first completion pays the whole stage count, so short workloads do not reach the ideal asymptotic throughput. Comparing against an unpipelined machine requires an explicit serial latency and the same workload. Extra stages may improve combinational balance while adding register overhead and branch recovery cost. Report whether a question asks total completion time, individual latency or steady-state completion interval, since these quantities can move in different directions.
''',
E('fundamental','Three pipeline stages have logic delays 2,5,3 ns; every pipeline register contributes 1 ns overhead. Find clock period, single-instruction latency and ideal completion interval.',r'Find slowest stage::T_{clk}=\max(2,5,3)+1=6\ ns::All stages share a clock that accommodates the largest stage plus register overhead.~Count one instruction’s stages::L=3(6)=18\ ns::The instruction advances through three separate cycles.~Use ideal steady issue::::After filling, one instruction can complete every 6 ns in a balanced single-issue schedule without stalls.', 'Clock=6 ns; individual latency=18 ns; ideal steady completion interval=6 ns.','The 2 ns and 3 ns stages wait for the common clock boundary; summing only their logic delays would ignore stage quantization and overhead.'),
E('application','A four-stage pipeline clocks at 2 ns and runs 20 instructions from empty, with three total bubble cycles. An unpipelined machine takes 8 ns per instruction. Find time and speedup.',r'Count ideal and stalled cycles::C=4+20-1+3=26::Add pipeline fill and the actual total bubbles once.~Convert pipeline time::T_p=26(2)=52\ ns::Every scheduled cycle uses the specified clock.~Compare complete serial workload::T_s=20(8)=160\ ns,\quad speedup=160/52=40/13::Both times include all twenty instructions.', 'Pipeline time=52 ns; speedup=40/13≈3.077.','With no bubbles the run would need 23 cycles; the three extra cycles add 6 ns rather than three penalties per instruction.'),
E('trap','A 10 ns operation is split first into five balanced stages, then ten balanced stages. Register overhead is 1 ns in each design. Does doubling stages double steady throughput?',r'Compute five-stage period::T_5=10/5+1=3\ ns::The overhead is added to the two-nanosecond logic slice.~Compute ten-stage period::T_{10}=10/10+1=2\ ns::One nanosecond of logic still pays one nanosecond of register overhead.~Compare throughputs::R_{10}/R_5=T_5/T_{10}=3/2::Throughput improves by 1.5, not two, even before hazards.', 'No; ideal steady throughput improves by only 1.5 under these delays.','Single-instruction latency also rises from 5×3=15 ns to 10×2=20 ns, showing why stage count alone does not determine all performance measures.'))

P('2-pipeline-hazards','Pipeline hazards',r'''
A hazard is a timing conflict in overlapping execution, not necessarily an incorrect instruction ordering in the program. Structural hazards contend for a resource, data hazards need a not-yet-available value, and control hazards depend on an unresolved next PC. Remedies must match the cause: duplicating memory ports does not supply an unavailable register value, and forwarding does not resolve a branch direction by itself. Draw stage occupancy and mark the stage where data is required under the implementation’s rules. Simple in-order pipelines with fixed read and write stages avoid some hazards that out-of-order machines must handle. State register-file half-cycle behavior, memory-port count and bypass paths before converting a dependence into a stall count.
''',
E('fundamental','In a five-stage IF,ID,EX,MEM,WB pipeline, one shared single-port memory serves both instruction fetch and load/store access. I₁ is a load; can I₄ fetch in cycle 4 without a conflict?',r'Locate load access::::With no earlier stalls, I₁ is in MEM during cycle 4 and requests the memory port.~Locate younger fetch::::I₄ would normally enter IF in that same cycle and request the same port.~Apply resource limit::::One port cannot service both requests simultaneously, so the schedule needs a structural stall or a separate memory resource.', 'No; the two requests create a structural hazard in cycle 4.','The registers may be independent and there may be full data forwarding; neither change removes this physical memory-port conflict.'),
E('application','A five-stage pipeline has no forwarding. Registers write in the first half of WB and read in the second half of ID. Trace ADD r1,r2,r3 followed by SUB r4,r1,r5, starting empty.',r'Trace producer::::ADD uses IF1,ID2,EX3,MEM4,WB5; r1 becomes readable during cycle 5.~Hold consumer until writeback::::SUB has IF2 and reaches ID3, but must remain through cycles 3 and 4 before reading r1 in ID5.~Complete consumer::::SUB then uses EX6,MEM7,WB8, inserting two bubbles beyond the ideal two-instruction completion at cycle 6.', 'Two data-hazard stall cycles; final completion is cycle 8.','Five stages and two instructions give six ideal cycles; waiting for the cycle-5 register read adds two, matching the explicit trace.'),
E('trap','In the same fixed-stage in-order pipeline, I₁ reads r2 and younger I₂ later writes r2. Does this create a write-after-read stall?',r'Place older read::::I₁ reads its register operands in ID, normally cycle 2 when it starts at IF1.~Place younger write::::I₂ writes only in its later WB stage, normally cycle 6 when it starts at IF2.~Compare temporal order::::The older read already occurred before the younger write, preserving the required old value without a WAR stall.', 'No WAR stall is needed in this stated fixed-stage in-order model.','The apparent register name overlap is real, but the stage ordering guarantees read-before-write; a reordered implementation could require additional handling.'))

P('2-pipeline-forwarding-stages','Forwarding',r'''
Forwarding chooses the youngest preceding instruction that has produced the required operand in time for the consumer. Matching a register number is necessary but not sufficient: the producer must actually write that register, its value must be ready and the selected bypass must reach the consuming stage. When multiple older instructions target the same register, a nearer valid result must take priority over a stale farther result. A scheduling gap can eliminate a load-use stall because memory finishes before the later consumer reaches EX. These examples assume a five-stage in-order pipeline, ALU results at the end of EX and load data at the end of MEM; same-cycle end results cannot be consumed at the beginning of EX.
''',
E('fundamental','With full EX-input forwarding, trace ADD r1,r2,r3; SUB r4,r1,r5; AND r6,r4,r7 in a five-stage pipeline from empty. ALU results are ready at end of EX.',r'Place first production::::ADD reaches EX3 and produces r1 at the end of cycle 3.~Forward consecutive dependencies::::SUB consumes r1 in EX4 and produces r4 at its end; AND consumes that r4 in EX5.~Count the unstalled run::C=5+3-1=7::Each result exists before the next instruction’s consuming EX cycle.', 'No data stalls are needed; the three instructions finish by cycle 7.','The two producer/consumer pairs cross adjacent EX cycles, each with an available EX/MEM forwarding result.'),
E('application','Trace LW r1,0(r0); ADD r8,r9,r10; AND r2,r1,r3 with full EX-input forwarding. The middle ADD is independent; memory returns load data at end of MEM.',r'Trace load readiness::::LW uses EX3 and MEM4, so r1 exists at the end of cycle 4.~Use the independent gap::::The middle ADD uses EX4, while the dependent AND is scheduled for EX5.~Forward the available load::::At EX5, load data from the preceding MEM4 is available through the MEM/WB path; no load-use bubble is required.', 'No data stall is needed; final completion is cycle 7.','The dependent instruction’s EX occurs one cycle after the load’s MEM, unlike an immediately following consumer whose EX would occur too early in cycle 4.'),
E('trap','I₁ and I₂ both write r1, producing values 10 and 20 in EX3 and EX4. I₃ consumes r1 in EX5. Both older pipeline registers match r1; may forwarding choose I₁’s value?',r'Identify architectural ordering::::I₂ is the most recent preceding write to r1, so I₃ must observe its value 20.~Locate ready candidates::::At cycle 5, I₂’s EX/MEM result and I₁’s farther MEM/WB result can both be available.~Apply priority rule::::Choose the nearer valid writer I₂ rather than selecting an arbitrary matching register tag.', 'Forward 20 from I₂; selecting 10 would violate sequential instruction semantics.','Executing the instructions serially leaves r1=20 before I₃, providing an independent reference for the forwarding choice.'))

P('2-pipeline-hazards','Branches',r'''
A branch changes control flow according to an instruction-defined condition and target computation. Prediction selects a provisional direction or target before resolution; its correctness is measured against the eventual architectural decision. A saturating predictor is a small state machine, so trace prediction from the old counter state before updating it with the actual outcome. A wrong prediction can flush younger work, but those wrong-path instructions do not become additional retired program instructions. Their cost appears as lost cycles under the recovery model. Distinguish taken rate, branch frequency and misprediction rate, and state the branch-resolution stage and prediction policy before inferring either the number of discarded instructions or the penalty.
''',
E('fundamental','A four-byte branch at byte address 40 uses next-PC-relative byte offset +12. It branches when r1=0, and r1 is currently zero. Find next architectural PC.',r'Evaluate condition::r1=0\Rightarrow taken::The instruction’s stated condition is true.~Choose reference PC::PC_{next}=44::The four-byte branch’s next instruction begins four bytes after address 40.~Apply byte offset::target=44+12=56::A taken branch selects the computed target rather than fall-through 44.', 'The next architectural PC is 56.','The target is twelve bytes beyond the supplied next-PC reference; a false condition would instead select address 44.'),
E('application','A two-bit saturating predictor uses 00,01 to predict not-taken and 10,11 to predict taken. It starts at 01, increments on taken and decrements on not-taken. Trace actual outcomes T,T,N,T.',r'Predict before first update::::State 01 predicts N, so the first T misses and increments to 10; state 10 then correctly predicts the second T and becomes 11.~Process the opposite outcome::::State 11 predicts T for the third outcome N, misses, and decrements to 10.~Finish and count::::State 10 correctly predicts the final T and becomes 11. Two of the four predictions were wrong.', 'Predictions are N,T,T,T; mispredictions=2/4=50%; final state=11.','Counter states after outcomes are 10,11,10,11. Predictions use their preceding states, preventing the common error of predicting after updating.'),
E('trap','A branch resolves at end of EX in a five-stage pipeline, flushing two younger wrong-path instructions in IF and ID. Should those two instructions increase the program’s retired instruction count?',r'Identify discarded work::::The younger IF/ID instructions belong to the incorrectly predicted path and are canceled before architectural completion.~Define retired instruction count::::Only instructions that complete on the architectural execution path count toward the executed program’s retired instruction total.~Account for performance cost::::The lost fetch/decode work contributes recovery cycles under the specified pipeline model, rather than two extra retired instructions.', 'No; the flushed instructions add recovery cost but do not count as retired program instructions.','An architectural instruction trace excludes the canceled operations, while a cycle trace records their wasted pipeline occupancy; the two measurements intentionally differ.'))
